  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-system1-observer",
      "text": "**Human's words:** This is vibe-coded. Agents could take initiative to, and are mostly free to gather rubric and evaluate using system1 call on parts or whole of sessions.\nAlmost all is volatile. Agent can change configurations which will be hot-loaded.\nMeasurement being important, the actions to make use of the evaluation are interesting and useful. \nThe \"seams\" are there to feedback to the human or agent, and the current state of this project provides a flexible basis."
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-system1-observer",
      "text": "Following is written by agents, self-evaluated and compared notes, with README writing standards they researched and formulated.\nThese words are results of two LLM in DeepSeek Harness and a TypeSafe jev model in dsh-system1-observer plugin.\n\n--------\n\n**A seam observer for the DeepSeek Harness.** It asks a System One model what the agent loop is doing — at the points\nyou choose — and writes every call, every skip and every answer to a JSONL trace."
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-system1-observer",
      "text": "**It decides nothing.** Each listener returns the loop's own decision *by reference*; every trace line says\n`enforcement: \"declarative\", verified: false`. Its product is evidence about the decision model's replies at each point\nof the loop, not judgements about the session it is observing."
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-system1-observer",
      "text": "**This page is short on purpose**: it is capped at 100 lines by `test/readme.test.js`, and the reference material —\nevery setting, the seams, the trace, and the measured claims **with the ones that are not verified** — is in the\n[manual](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/manual.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```bash\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-system1-observer\n```\n\n`web` is the profile to install into — substitute the name of yours. Or from a clone, which needs no npm account:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer.git\ncd dsh-system1-observer && ./install.sh web\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "It needs the profile's harness for `@deepseek-ai/cordis` and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools` (they are **peerDependencies**,\nnot bundled), and a model to call: the profile's `system1` service, or the HTTP wire at `wireUrl` (default\n`http://127.0.0.1:8766`)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Sixty seconds",
      "text": "```\nsystem1_explain                                    what it can do, what it is set to now, what a reading covers\nsession_index_search { query: 'XYZ' }                    find the session  (dsh-session-index)\nsystem1_evaluate_session { sessionId: '…', segmentChars: 57600, package: true }   judge it, and package the run\nsystem1_measurements { action: 'interpret', package: '…', text: '…', by: 'you' }  attach your reading"
    },
    {
      "section": "Sixty seconds",
      "text": "`system1_evaluate_session` does the mechanical work in one call: it reads the session, segments it if it is large, asks\nevery question of every segment, aggregates in code and writes a report package. The last step is a separate call, and\ndeliberately yours — prose cannot be derived from numbers."
    },
    {
      "section": "Sixty seconds",
      "text": "Nine tools in all: `system1_explain`, `system1_settings`, `system1_decide`, `system1_evaluate_session`,\n`system1_measurements`, `system1_session_subject`, `system1_question_sets`, `system1_battery`, `system1_trace`. Finding a session is `dsh-session-index`'s — `session_index_list`/`search`/`refresh`, in milliseconds."
    },
    {
      "section": "What you get",
      "text": "- **The trace**: `~/.dsh/logs/system1-observer.jsonl` — one line per call, per skip and per start-up, read by the\n  `system1_trace` tool, the trace card, or `scripts/trace.mjs`.\n- **The settings card** on the Plugins page: every live-writable setting, and \"…\" → **Observe this session**.\n- **A written record**: the [register](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/findings.md)\n  notes every problem this instrument has had and the measurement that found it."
    },
    {
      "section": "Before you trust a reading",
      "text": "1. **Long conversations are summarised** unless you pass `segmentChars` — a large session needs segments.\n2. **Where it has no question of its own, it records that it did not ask** rather than guessing.\n3. **Its built-in question is measurably weaker at the tool-call points**, so a tool-heavy run reads differently.\n4. **Readings either side of a configuration change are not comparable** — every change is written to the trace first."
    },
    {
      "section": "Before you trust a reading",
      "text": "`system1_explain` prints these in full, derived from the running row."
    },
    {
      "section": "Working on it",
      "text": "```bash\nnpm run ci    # the six gates in order: test, coverage, citations, compat, composition, deploy\n```\n\nThe rules this repository holds itself to, each with the measurement that produced it, are in the [conventions](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/conventions.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Questions, and contributing",
      "text": "Ask in [GitHub issues](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/issues); pull requests are welcome. How a release\nhappens, and what it does not ship, is the maintainer's runbook, kept off-repository with the other notes."
    },
    {
      "section": "Documentation",
      "text": "| | |\n|---|---|\n| [the chart](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/architecture.png) | the whole row in one picture: the data path from the loop to the trace, the nine tools, the repository around it |\n| [manual](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/manual.md) | the reference: hooks, every setting, the trace, mounting, the measured claims and the unverified ones |\n| [docs/settings.md](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/settings.md) | every setting: what it does, what it costs, why it exists |\n| [docs/measurement-depth.md](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/measurement-depth.md) | what evidence a judgement gets (G0–G4) |\n| [docs/question-suitability.md](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/question-suitability.md) | which question type fits which evidence |\n| [case study](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/docs/case-study-readme-review.md) | two agents, one rubric and this page's own redesign, measured — the method, the numbers and the mistakes |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Licence",
      "text": "MIT — see [LICENSE](https://github.com/johnlam1968/dsh-system1-observer/blob/master/LICENSE)."
    }
  ],
